WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Do you remember the first house he [Valery Montgomery] moved into in Mossville?

2
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.580
I would assume right down the street from here.

3
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:13.540
It was a . . . The address was 401 Prater Road.

4
00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:20.360
It used to be Route One I think . . . route . . . I think its Route One, Box 401.

5
00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.040
Is that the same address as the Paradise Club?

6
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.040
Yeah.

7
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.280
It was right across the street.

8
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:28.460
Had a balcony on top.

9
00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:30.380
Oh, the club?

10
00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:31.420
Yeah.

11
00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.200
Yeah the . . . They had two parts.

12
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:41.300
They had, like, a club part in the front and then they built . . . Later on, they built a dance hall.

13
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.260
They wanted to have, you know, something so the people to come for entertainment.

14
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:46.140
Had a stage.

15
00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:54.400
Big groups, a band . . . so that was some big entertainers came through.

16
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.440
I’m telling you.

17
00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.340
What do y’all remember about the Paradise Club?

18
00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:04.480
I remember everything. [laughs] That was our little stomping ground.

19
00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.560
That’s where everybody went, to The Paradise.

20
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:12.640
And he had a lot of concerts back in the days.

21
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:18.840
We’d get to see all of the artists, the singers, you know.

22
00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.240
That place was popping.

23
00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.040
Tina Turner, Bobby Blue Bland, James Brown.

24
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.820
James Brown.

25
00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:26.100
Otis Redding.

26
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.980
We’ve seen all of them here.

27
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.060
Al Green.

28
00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:32.220
Yeah, right there at the Paradise.

29
00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:42.360
Then they had a . . . like a little café and they would cook, like, short orders.

30
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.760
I know my daddy brother, Wes, would barbeque and people would love his barbeque.

31
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.240
He was a good cook.

32
00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:56.120
Then mama and them had the club part and they would sell the liquor and stuff . . . You’d hear all kind of stuff going on.

33
00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:57.313
But a lot of teens.

34
00:01:57.313 --> 00:02:03.000
There was more teens there than anything on weekends.

35
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.600
Didn’t have too much older people there.

36
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:13.020
During the weekdays, you know, daytime sometimes, but most of the time the weekends was the teenagers there.

37
00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:18.160
So what was a typical Saturday night like?

38
00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.320
They would all come in kind of late around eleven.

39
00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:24.700
Starting at eleven o’clock.

40
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.180
Then they would start coming in.

41
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:35.540
And they danced until they get tired and then mama would say, “Alright it’s time to shut it down,” stuff like that.

42
00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.060
So they’d have a good time.

43
00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:41.000
And everybody liked to go to The Paradise.

44
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.700
Go there and have a good time.

45
00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:44.780
Didn’t have no fighting and all of that.

46
00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.780
No shooting.

47
00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:50.320
They come from everywhere.

48
00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.080
It was a good place to be.

49
00:02:53.080 --> 00:03:01.240
You can go there and enjoy yourself and not have to worry about the . . . Nobody getting hurt.

50
00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.560
You can walk outside and go to your car.

51
00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.200
You didn’t have to worry about that either.

52
00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:06.940
They didn’t have the drugs . . .

53
00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:09.140
And sometimes some of them thought they was a good dancer,

54
00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:14.640
they would take turns trying to out-show the other one like, “Oh I got this move.”

55
00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:14.960
They had dance contests.

56
00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:15.980
They had plenty of them.

57
00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:16.600
[Agrees].

58
00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:17.440
I got this move . . .

59
00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.320
I won some of them.

60
00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:30.460
And they used to give out albums, tickets to something, they had another place in Lake Charles that was Paul’s Auditorium.

61
00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:36.000
Sometimes you could get tickets for that or tickets to different things.

62
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.380
How did they advertise the musicians that were going to be there?

63
00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:41.500
They’d have it on the radio.

64
00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.080
And then they’d have it on posters.

65
00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.060
[Agrees].

66
00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:49.860
My daddy would have us making some posters and they’d go tack them on the telephone trees.

67
00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:50.340
And posters . . .

68
00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:56.620
On the posters and on the stores, on the front of the stores, the stations.

69
00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:57.080
You see them.

70
00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.900
We didn’t have a Ticketmaster then.

71
00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.720
We didn’t have nowhere to buy no ticket.

72
00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:06.120
You come get them here, or you get them at the door, or whoever was selling them.

73
00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.200
They’d get different ones to sell them for them.

74
00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:11.260
And they was so cheap, but it was high back then.

75
00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:12.340
We thought it was high.

76
00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.800
Three dollars.

77
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.020
You can’t even see a concert for three dollars now.

78
00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:19.880
No, no. that’s different now.

79
00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:23.047
Can’t even buy a Coke in the concert for three dollars.
